@lang en
IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `usca-operation-of-statutes`

-- California rules of reading: the canons of construction of the Government Code, the Civil Code and the Code of Civil Procedure,
-- each encoded as a function over a small typed representation of what the rule operates on (a part of the text, a tense, a gender, a number,
-- a word that has or has not a technical meaning), with the open-textured judgment taken as a plain input.
--
-- Added in pass 2 of the completion to "whole".  Pass 1 left these inert as "canons"; the brief says an interpretive canon is encoded as a rule.
-- Sources: Gov. Code §§ 6, 10, 11, 12, 12.5, 13, 23, 24, 9603; Civ. Code §§ 4, 13, 14(a), 14(b)(6), 22, 22.1; Code Civ. Proc. §§ 4, 16, 17(a), 17(b)(9).
-- The quotation lines are copied by script from the converter-made plain-text rendering of the sections (not the official text) and checked
-- against it by srcrefs.py.  A case the text does not answer is a named REFUSE, never FALSE.  Target: unstable (REFUSE).
-- Imports: prelude and usca-operation-of-statutes (for `Kind of code`).

§ `Gov. Code § 6: headings`

DECLARE `Element of the text` IS ONE OF
    `a title heading`
    `a division heading`
    `a part heading`
    `a chapter heading`
    `an article heading`
    `a section heading`
    `a subdivision heading`
    `a paragraph heading`
    `the operative text of a provision`

-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:18 | 6. Title, division, part, chapter, article, and section headings do not in any manner affect the scope, meaning, or intent of the provisions of this code.
@ref Gov. Code § 6 (src:GOV-ss-1-26:18)
GIVEN element IS AN `Element of the text`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the element may affect the scope, meaning or intent of the provisions of the Government Code` element MEANS
    CONSIDER element
    WHEN `a title heading` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a division heading` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a part heading` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a chapter heading` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `an article heading` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a section heading` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a subdivision heading` THEN REFUSE "Gov. Code § 6 names title, division, part, chapter, article and section headings; it does not say whether the heading of a subdivision affects the scope, meaning or intent of the provisions of the code"
    WHEN `a paragraph heading` THEN REFUSE "Gov. Code § 6 names title, division, part, chapter, article and section headings; it does not say whether the heading of a paragraph affects the scope, meaning or intent of the provisions of the code"
    WHEN `the operative text of a provision` THEN REFUSE "Gov. Code § 6 says only that headings do not affect the scope, meaning or intent of the provisions of the code; it does not say what the operative text does"

§ `Gov. Code § 10, Civ. Code § 14(b)(6), Code Civ. Proc. § 17(b)(9): section and subdivision`

DECLARE `What a reference word points at` IS ONE OF
    `a section of this code`
    `a section of the other code or statute`
    `a subdivision of the section in which the term occurs`
    `a subdivision of the section expressly mentioned`

-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:45 | 10. “Section” means a section of this code unless some other statute is specifically mentioned. “Subdivision” means a subdivision of the section in which the term occurs unless some other section is expressly mentioned.
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:66 | (6) The word “section” whenever hereinafter employed refers to a section of this code, unless some other code or statute is expressly mentioned.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:74 | (9) “Section” refers to a section of this code, unless some other code or
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:75 | statute is expressly mentioned.
-- Gov. Code § 10 says "specifically" and Civ. Code § 14(b)(6) and Code Civ. Proc. § 17(b)(9) say "expressly"; the three sections are applied alike.
-- A mention of another code is a mention of another statute for Gov. Code § 10 (each code is an Act: Gov. Code § 1).  Civ. Code § 14(b) and
-- Code Civ. Proc. § 17(b) apply "unless otherwise apparent from the context"; that limit is taken as the input `the context otherwise shows`.
@ref Gov. Code § 10, Civ. Code § 14(b)(6), Code Civ. Proc. § 17(b)(9) (src:GOV-ss-1-26:45, src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:66, src:CCP-ss-1-34:74-75)
GIVEN `the reference mentions some other code or statute` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `What a reference word points at`
`the section that the word section refers to when` `the reference mentions some other code or statute` MEANS
    IF `the reference mentions some other code or statute`
    THEN `a section of the other code or statute`
    ELSE `a section of this code`

@ref Gov. Code § 10 (src:GOV-ss-1-26:45)
GIVEN `the reference mentions some other section` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `What a reference word points at`
`the subdivision that the word subdivision refers to when` `the reference mentions some other section` MEANS
    IF `the reference mentions some other section`
    THEN `a subdivision of the section expressly mentioned`
    ELSE `a subdivision of the section in which the term occurs`

§ `Tense, gender and number: Gov. Code §§ 11-13, Civ. Code § 14(a), Code Civ. Proc. § 17(a)`

DECLARE `Grammatical tense` IS ONE OF
    `the past tense`
    `the present tense`
    `the future tense`

DECLARE `Grammatical gender` IS ONE OF
    `the masculine gender`
    `the feminine gender`
    `the neuter gender`

DECLARE `Grammatical number` IS ONE OF
    `the singular number`
    `the plural number`

-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:47 | 11. The present tense includes the past and future tenses; and the future, the present.
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:57 | 14. (a) Words used in this code in the present tense include the future as well as the present; words used in the masculine gender include the feminine and neuter; the singular number includes the plural, and the plural the singular; the word person includes a corporation as well as a natural person; county includes city and county; writing includes printing and typewriting; oath includes affirmation or
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:58 | 17. (a) Words used in this code in the present tense include the future as well as the present. Words used in the masculine gender include the feminine and neuter. The singular number includes the plural and the plural number includes the singular.
-- Gov. Code § 11: "The present tense includes the past and future tenses; and the future, the present."  Civ. Code § 14(a) and Code Civ. Proc. § 17(a):
-- words in the present tense "include the future as well as the present", and nothing is said of the past.  The three differ on the past tense
-- (fork F-tense), so the function takes the code.  A pair of tenses the code does not mention is refused, not FALSE.
@ref Gov. Code § 11, Civ. Code § 14(a), Code Civ. Proc. § 17(a) (src:GOV-ss-1-26:47, src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:57, src:CCP-ss-1-34:58)
GIVEN code IS A `Kind of code`
      used IS A `Grammatical tense`
      included IS A `Grammatical tense`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a word used in the tense includes the tense, in` code used included MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF used EQUALS included THEN TRUE
        IF code EQUALS `the Government Code`
            THEN CONSIDER used
                 WHEN `the present tense` THEN TRUE
                 WHEN `the future tense` THEN
                     IF included EQUALS `the present tense`
                     THEN TRUE
                     ELSE REFUSE "Gov. Code § 11 says the present tense includes the past and future tenses and the future the present; it does not say whether the future tense includes the past"
                 WHEN `the past tense` THEN REFUSE "Gov. Code § 11 says the present tense includes the past and future tenses and the future the present; it does not say what the past tense includes"
        IF used EQUALS `the present tense` AND included EQUALS `the future tense`
            THEN TRUE
        OTHERWISE REFUSE "Civ. Code § 14(a) and Code Civ. Proc. § 17(a) say words in the present tense include the future as well as the present; they say nothing of the past tense or of the future tense including the present"

-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:49 | 12. The masculine gender includes the feminine and neuter.
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:57 | 14. (a) Words used in this code in the present tense include the future as well as the present; words used in the masculine gender include the feminine and neuter; the singular number includes the plural, and the plural the singular; the word person includes a corporation as well as a natural person; county includes city and county; writing includes printing and typewriting; oath includes affirmation or
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:58 | 17. (a) Words used in this code in the present tense include the future as well as the present. Words used in the masculine gender include the feminine and neuter. The singular number includes the plural and the plural number includes the singular.
@ref Gov. Code § 12, Civ. Code § 14(a), Code Civ. Proc. § 17(a) (src:GOV-ss-1-26:49, src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:57, src:CCP-ss-1-34:58)
GIVEN code IS A `Kind of code`
      used IS A `Grammatical gender`
      included IS A `Grammatical gender`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a word used in the gender includes the gender, in` code used included MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF used EQUALS included THEN TRUE
        IF used EQUALS `the masculine gender` THEN TRUE
        OTHERWISE REFUSE "Gov. Code § 12, Civ. Code § 14(a) and Code Civ. Proc. § 17(a) say the masculine gender includes the feminine and neuter; they say nothing of the feminine or the neuter including another gender"

-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:55 | 13. The singular number includes the plural, and the plural the singular.
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:57 | 14. (a) Words used in this code in the present tense include the future as well as the present; words used in the masculine gender include the feminine and neuter; the singular number includes the plural, and the plural the singular; the word person includes a corporation as well as a natural person; county includes city and county; writing includes printing and typewriting; oath includes affirmation or
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:58 | 17. (a) Words used in this code in the present tense include the future as well as the present. Words used in the masculine gender include the feminine and neuter. The singular number includes the plural and the plural number includes the singular.
@ref Gov. Code § 13, Civ. Code § 14(a), Code Civ. Proc. § 17(a) (src:GOV-ss-1-26:55, src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:57, src:CCP-ss-1-34:58)
GIVEN code IS A `Kind of code`
      used IS A `Grammatical number`
      included IS A `Grammatical number`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a word used in the number includes the number, in` code used included MEANS
    TRUE

§ `Words and phrases: Civ. Code § 13, Code Civ. Proc. § 16`

DECLARE `Source of the meaning of a word or phrase` IS ONE OF
    `the context and the approved usage of the language`
    `the peculiar and appropriate meaning in law, or the definition in the succeeding section`

-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:55 | 13. Words and phrases are construed according to the context and the approved usage of the language; but technical words and phrases, and such others as may have acquired a peculiar and appropriate meaning in law, or are defined in the succeeding section, are to be construed according to such peculiar and appropriate meaning or definition.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:56 | 16. Words and phrases are construed according to the context and the approved usage of the language; but technical words and phrases, and such others as have acquired a peculiar and appropriate meaning in law, or are defined in the succeeding section, are to be construed according to such peculiar and appropriate meaning or definition.
-- "technical words and phrases, and such others as may have acquired a peculiar and appropriate meaning in law, or are defined in the succeeding
-- section": whether a word is technical or has acquired a peculiar meaning is the open-textured judgment, taken as an input.
@ref Civ. Code § 13, Code Civ. Proc. § 16 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:55, src:CCP-ss-1-34:56)
GIVEN code IS A `Kind of code`
      `the word or phrase is technical or has acquired a peculiar and appropriate meaning in law` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is defined in the succeeding section` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Source of the meaning of a word or phrase`
`the source of the meaning of a word or phrase, in` code `given that it is technical or peculiar:` `the word or phrase is technical or has acquired a peculiar and appropriate meaning in law` `and that it is defined in the succeeding section:` `it is defined in the succeeding section` MEANS
    IF code EQUALS `the Government Code`
    THEN REFUSE "no deposited section of the Government Code says how words and phrases are construed; Civ. Code § 13 and Code Civ. Proc. § 16 are the sections that do"
    ELSE IF `the word or phrase is technical or has acquired a peculiar and appropriate meaning in law` OR `it is defined in the succeeding section`
         THEN `the peculiar and appropriate meaning in law, or the definition in the succeeding section`
         ELSE `the context and the approved usage of the language`

§ `Strict and liberal construction: Civ. Code § 4, Code Civ. Proc. § 4`

DECLARE `Approach to construction` IS ONE OF
    strict
    liberal

DECLARE `What is construed` IS ONE OF
    `a provision of the code`
    `a proceeding under the code`
    `a statute in derogation of the common law that is outside the code`

-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:18 | 4. The rule of the common law, that statutes in derogation thereof are to be strictly construed, has no application to this Code. The Code establishes the law of this State respecting the subjects to which it relates, and its provisions are to be liberally construed with a view to effect its objects and to promote justice.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:18 | 4. The rule of the common law, that statutes in derogation thereof are to be strictly construed, has no application to this Code. The Code establishes the law of this State respecting the subjects to which it relates, and its provisions and all proceedings under it are to be liberally construed, with a view to effect its objects and to promote justice.
-- Civ. Code § 4: "The rule of the common law, that statutes in derogation thereof are to be strictly construed, has no application to this Code."
-- The Government Code has no such section in the deposit.
@ref Civ. Code § 4, Code Civ. Proc. § 4 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:18, src:CCP-ss-1-34:18)
GIVEN code IS A `Kind of code`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the common-law rule of strict construction of statutes in derogation of it applies to the code` code MEANS
    CONSIDER code
    WHEN `the Civil Code` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `the Code of Civil Procedure` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `the Government Code` THEN REFUSE "the Government Code has no deposited section on the common-law rule of strict construction; Civ. Code § 4 and Code Civ. Proc. § 4 speak of their own codes"

@ref Civ. Code § 4, Code Civ. Proc. § 4 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:18, src:CCP-ss-1-34:18)
GIVEN what IS A `What is construed`
      code IS A `Kind of code`
GIVETH AN `Approach to construction`
`the approach to construing` what `in` code MEANS
    CONSIDER code
    WHEN `the Government Code` THEN REFUSE "the Government Code has no deposited section that says how its provisions are to be construed in this sense"
    WHEN `the Civil Code` THEN
        CONSIDER what
        WHEN `a provision of the code` THEN liberal
        WHEN `a proceeding under the code` THEN REFUSE "Civ. Code § 4 says its provisions are to be liberally construed; it does not mention proceedings under the code"
        WHEN `a statute in derogation of the common law that is outside the code` THEN REFUSE "Civ. Code § 4 speaks of the Civil Code only"
    WHEN `the Code of Civil Procedure` THEN
        CONSIDER what
        WHEN `a provision of the code` THEN liberal
        WHEN `a proceeding under the code` THEN liberal
        WHEN `a statute in derogation of the common law that is outside the code` THEN REFUSE "Code Civ. Proc. § 4 speaks of the Code of Civil Procedure and proceedings under it only"

-- "The Code establishes the law of this State respecting the subjects to which it relates."
@ref Civ. Code § 4, Code Civ. Proc. § 4 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:18, src:CCP-ss-1-34:18)
GIVEN code IS A `Kind of code`
      `the subject is one to which the code relates` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the code establishes the law of this State on the subject` code `given that the subject is one to which the code relates:` `the subject is one to which the code relates` MEANS
    IF code EQUALS `the Government Code`
    THEN REFUSE "the Government Code has no deposited section that says it establishes the law of this State on the subjects to which it relates"
    ELSE IF `the subject is one to which the code relates`
         THEN TRUE
         ELSE REFUSE "Civ. Code § 4 and Code Civ. Proc. § 4 speak of the subjects to which the code relates; they say nothing of another subject"

§ `Gov. Code § 23: severability`

DECLARE `What is held invalid` IS ONE OF
    `a provision of the code`
    `the application of a provision to a person or circumstance`

-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:88 | 23. If any provision of this code, or the application thereof to any person or circumstance, is held invalid, the remainder of the code, or the application of such provision to other persons or circumstances, shall not be affected thereby.
@ref Gov. Code § 23 (src:GOV-ss-1-26:88)
GIVEN held IS A `What is held invalid`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the remainder of the code is affected when held invalid is` held MEANS
    CONSIDER held
    WHEN `a provision of the code` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `the application of a provision to a person or circumstance` THEN REFUSE "Gov. Code § 23 says that when a provision is held invalid the remainder of the code is not affected, and that when its application to a person or circumstance is held invalid its application to others is not affected; it does not say the same of the remainder of the code in the second case"

@ref Gov. Code § 23 (src:GOV-ss-1-26:88)
GIVEN held IS A `What is held invalid`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the application of the provision to other persons or circumstances is affected when held invalid is` held MEANS
    CONSIDER held
    WHEN `the application of a provision to a person or circumstance` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a provision of the code` THEN REFUSE "Gov. Code § 23 says that when the application of a provision to a person or circumstance is held invalid its application to others is not affected; for a provision held invalid it says only that the remainder of the code is not affected"

§ `Gov. Code § 12.5 and § 24: the words man and workmen's compensation`

-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:53 | 12.5. The Legislature hereby declares its intent that the terms “man” or “men” where appropriate shall be deemed “person” or “persons” and any references to the terms “man” or “men” in sections of this code be changed to “person” or “persons” when such code sections are being amended for any purpose. This act is declaratory and not amendatory of existing law.
-- "the terms 'man' or 'men' where appropriate shall be deemed 'person' or 'persons'": whether it is appropriate is the open-textured judgment, an input.
-- The last sentence, "This act is declaratory and not amendatory of existing law", is not operative.
@ref Gov. Code § 12.5 (src:GOV-ss-1-26:53)
GIVEN term IS A STRING
      `it is appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A STRING
`the term deemed in place of` term `where it is appropriate:` `it is appropriate` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `it is appropriate`
            THEN REFUSE "Gov. Code § 12.5 deems the terms man and men to be person and persons where appropriate; it does not say how a term is read where it is not appropriate"
        IF term EQUALS "man" THEN "person"
        IF term EQUALS "men" THEN "persons"
        OTHERWISE REFUSE "Gov. Code § 12.5 names the terms man and men only"

@ref Gov. Code § 12.5 (src:GOV-ss-1-26:53)
GIVEN `the section is being amended for any purpose` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a reference to the term man or men is to be changed to person or persons` `the section is being amended for any purpose` MEANS
    `the section is being amended for any purpose`

-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:90 | 24. The Legislature hereby declares its intent that the term “workmen’s compensation” shall hereafter also be known as “workers’ compensation.” In furtherance of this policy it is the desire of the Legislature that references to the term “workmen’s compensation” in this code be changed to “workers’ compensation” when such code sections are being amended for any purpose. This act is declaratory and not amendatory of existing law.
@ref Gov. Code § 24 (src:GOV-ss-1-26:90)
GIVEN term IS A STRING
GIVETH A STRING
`the term also known as` term MEANS
    IF term EQUALS "workmen's compensation"
    THEN "workers' compensation"
    ELSE REFUSE "Gov. Code § 24 names the term workmen's compensation only"

@ref Gov. Code § 24 (src:GOV-ss-1-26:90)
GIVEN `the section is being amended for any purpose` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a reference to workmen's compensation is to be changed to workers' compensation` `the section is being amended for any purpose` MEANS
    `the section is being amended for any purpose`

§ `Civ. Code § 22 and § 22.1: law and the will of the supreme power`

DECLARE `Instrument of the State` IS ONE OF
    `the Constitution`
    `a statute`
    `something else`

-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:82 | 22. Law is a solemn expression of the will of the supreme power of the State.
-- "Law is a solemn expression of the will of the supreme power of the State": read as a definition, so both limbs are needed (fork F-22).
@ref Civ. Code § 22 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:82)
GIVEN `it is a solemn expression` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is an expression of the will of the supreme power of the State` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`an expression is a law` `it is a solemn expression` `it is an expression of the will of the supreme power of the State` MEANS
    `it is a solemn expression` AND `it is an expression of the will of the supreme power of the State`

-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:84 | 22.1. The will of the supreme power is expressed:
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:85 | (a) By the Constitution.
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:86 | (b) By statutes.
@ref Civ. Code § 22.1 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:84-86)
GIVEN instrument IS AN `Instrument of the State`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the will of the supreme power is expressed by` instrument MEANS
    CONSIDER instrument
    WHEN `the Constitution` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a statute` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `something else` THEN REFUSE "Civ. Code § 22.1 says the will of the supreme power is expressed by the Constitution and by statutes; it does not say that nothing else expresses it"

§ `Gov. Code § 9603: where the general rules are`

-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:13 | 9603. The general rules for the construction of statutes are contained in the preliminary provisions of the different codes.
@ref Gov. Code § 9603 (src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:13)
GIVEN code IS A `Kind of code`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the preliminary provisions of the code contain general rules for the construction of statutes` code MEANS
    TRUE

@ref Gov. Code § 9603 (src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:13)
GIVETH A STRING
`the general rules for the construction of a statute that is in no code are in` MEANS
    REFUSE "Gov. Code § 9603 says the general rules are in the preliminary provisions of the different codes; it says nothing of a statute that is in no code"
